ADDART({id:"ariane",t:"Ariane Rockets",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇪🇺",w:"Ariane (rocket family)",r:["spaceagencies","rocket","jwst","spaceports","rosetta"],
p:[["Europe’s rockets","Ariane rockets give Europe its own access to space."],["Launched from the jungle","They lift off from French Guiana in South America."],["Webb’s ride","An Ariane 5 launched the James Webb Space Telescope."]],
l:"Ariane is a family of European rockets developed for the European Space Agency (ESA) and operated by the company Arianespace, giving Europe independent access to space. The program began in the 1970s after Europe struggled to get its satellites launched by others. Ariane 1 first flew on 24 December 1979 from the Guiana Space Centre in Kourou, French Guiana, a site close to the equator, where Earth’s rotation gives rockets an extra boost. Ariane rockets became leaders in launching commercial communications satellites in the 1980s and 1990s. Ariane 5, first launched in 1996, carried many famous missions, including the Rosetta comet probe in 2004 and the James Webb Space Telescope on 25 December 2021, and flew for the last time in July 2023. Its successor, Ariane 6, made its first flight on 9 July 2024. Ariane 6 comes in two versions, with two or four strap-on boosters; the more powerful Ariane 64 first flew on 12 February 2026, carrying 32 satellites for Amazon’s Leo internet network. Europe faces strong competition from SpaceX’s reusable rockets and is studying reusable designs.",
s:[["Kourou","The Guiana Space Centre lies about 5 degrees north of the equator. Launching eastward from near the equator takes advantage of Earth’s rotation, which saves fuel. Its location also allows rockets to launch over the ocean."],["Ariane 5’s first flight","On 4 June 1996, the first Ariane 5 veered off course and self-destructed 37 seconds after launch. A software error, caused by converting a large number into a format too small to hold it, became one of the most famous bugs in computing history."],["Launching Webb","Ariane 5 placed the James Webb Space Telescope on such an accurate path that Webb saved fuel, extending its expected lifetime to perhaps 20 years."],["Ariane 6","Ariane 6 was designed to be cheaper than Ariane 5, with an upper stage that can restart several times to deliver satellites to different orbits. Its debut followed years of delays, during which Europe briefly had no rocket of its own for large satellites."]],
k:[["Operator","Arianespace"],["Launch site","Guiana Space Centre, Kourou, French Guiana"],["First Ariane launch","24 December 1979"],["Ariane 5","1996–2023"],["Ariane 6 first flight","9 July 2024"]],
d:[["1979","Ariane 1 first flight"],["1980","Arianespace founded"],["1996","Ariane 5 first flight fails"],["2004","Ariane 5 launches Rosetta"],["2021","Ariane 5 launches the James Webb Space Telescope"],["2024","Ariane 6 first flight"],["2026","First Ariane 64 launch"]],
s2:[["The name","Ariane is the French form of Ariadne, the princess in Greek mythology who helped Theseus escape the labyrinth. It was chosen in 1973 by the French minister responsible for the program."],["Vega","Europe also operates Vega, a smaller rocket for lighter satellites. Its upgraded version, Vega-C, is now operated by the Italian company Avio."]],
sp:["Webb’s Christmas launch","The James Webb Space Telescope, the most expensive science mission ever, lifted off on an Ariane 5 on Christmas Day 2021, a launch watched by millions around the world."],
g:[["Launch vehicle","A rocket that carries payloads to space"],["Strap-on booster","An extra rocket attached to the side"],["Upper stage","The top part of a rocket that reaches orbit"],["Commercial satellite","A satellite owned by a company"]],
f:["Ariane 1 first flew in 1979.","Ariane rockets launch from French Guiana.","An Ariane 5 launched the Webb telescope."],
fun:"A single software bug destroyed the first Ariane 5 rocket only 37 seconds after launch in 1996."});

ADDART({id:"asteroidmining",t:"Asteroid Mining",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⛏️",w:"Asteroid mining",r:["asteroidbelt","psyche","osirisrex","hayabusa","spacelaw"],
p:[["Mines in space","Asteroid mining means extracting materials from asteroids."],["Water and metals","Asteroids contain water, iron, nickel and precious metals."],["Not yet","No one has mined an asteroid yet, but missions have collected samples."]],
l:"Asteroid mining is the idea of extracting useful materials from asteroids, the rocky and metallic leftovers from the formation of the solar system. Some asteroids contain water ice, which could be turned into drinking water, oxygen and rocket fuel for spacecraft, and others are rich in iron, nickel, cobalt and platinum-group metals. Supporters argue that mining in space could supply materials for building in orbit and on the Moon without lifting them from Earth, reduce the need for harmful mining on Earth and create new industries. Space missions have shown that reaching and sampling asteroids is possible: Japan’s Hayabusa and Hayabusa2 returned tiny samples from the asteroids Itokawa and Ryugu, and NASA’s OSIRIS-REx brought about 121.6 grams of material from the asteroid Bennu to Earth in 2023. NASA’s Psyche mission, launched in October 2023, is traveling to a metal-rich asteroid. However, mining asteroids on a large scale faces huge challenges, including the cost of reaching them, working in very low gravity and processing materials in space. Early asteroid-mining companies in the 2010s went out of business, and the legal rules for owning resources from space are still debated.",
s:[["Types of asteroids","Carbon-rich C-type asteroids, the most common, contain water locked in minerals and organic compounds. Stony S-type asteroids contain silicate rock and some metal. Rarer M-type asteroids, such as Psyche, may be largely iron and nickel."],["Water first","Many experts think water will be the first space resource worth mining, because it is expensive to launch from Earth. Split into hydrogen and oxygen, it can fuel rockets, making asteroids and the Moon potential ‘gas stations’ in space."],["Who owns it?","The 1967 Outer Space Treaty bans countries from claiming celestial bodies but does not clearly address resources. The United States in 2015 and Luxembourg in 2017 passed laws allowing companies to own resources they extract, and the Artemis Accords support resource use, but some countries disagree."],["Companies","Planetary Resources and Deep Space Industries attracted attention in the 2010s but were sold by 2019. Newer startups such as AstroForge have launched small test missions, so far without reaching an asteroid."]],
k:[["Valuable resources","Water, iron, nickel, platinum-group metals"],["Bennu sample","About 121.6 grams (OSIRIS-REx, 2023)"],["Metal asteroid mission","Psyche (launched 2023)"],["US law allowing ownership","2015"],["Status","No commercial mining yet"]],
d:[["2010","Hayabusa returns samples from Itokawa"],["2015","US law on space resource ownership"],["2017","Luxembourg passes space resources law"],["2020","Hayabusa2 returns samples from Ryugu"],["2023","OSIRIS-REx returns samples from Bennu; Psyche launched"]],
s2:[["Trillion-dollar asteroids","Headlines sometimes claim that asteroids like Psyche are worth quadrillions of dollars. Such figures are misleading, because bringing so much metal to Earth would crash its price, and the cost of mining is unknown."],["Science first","Even if mining never pays off, asteroid samples are scientifically valuable. Ryugu and Bennu samples contained water-bearing minerals and organic molecules, including amino acids and all five bases found in DNA and RNA."]],
sp:["Mining in science fiction","Asteroid mining has long been a theme of science fiction, from early 20th-century stories to the TV series The Expanse, where miners in the asteroid belt form their own society."],
g:[["Space resources","Materials found in space that could be used"],["Platinum-group metals","Rare metals including platinum and palladium"],["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"],["Outer Space Treaty","The 1967 treaty forming the basis of space law"]],
f:["No asteroid has been commercially mined yet.","OSIRIS-REx brought back about 121.6 grams of Bennu.","Water may be the first space resource mined."],
fun:"Water mined from asteroids could someday be turned into rocket fuel, creating gas stations in space."});

ADDART({id:"spacesolar",t:"Space-Based Solar Power",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔆",w:"Space-based solar power",r:["solarpower","sat","renew","moonbases","orbits"],
p:[["Sunshine from orbit","Space-based solar power would collect sunlight in space and beam it to Earth."],["No night, no clouds","In orbit, solar panels can catch sunlight almost all the time."],["Early tests","Small experiments have beamed power in space."]],
l:"Space-based solar power is a proposed way of generating electricity by collecting sunlight with large solar panels in orbit and transmitting the energy to Earth, usually as microwaves or laser beams, where receiving antennas would convert it back into electricity. In space, there is no night for satellites in high orbits much of the time, no clouds and no atmosphere to weaken sunlight, so a panel can collect several times more energy than the same panel on the ground. The idea appeared in Isaac Asimov’s 1941 science fiction story ‘Reason’, and engineer Peter Glaser patented a detailed design in 1973. NASA and the US Department of Energy studied it in the 1970s but found it far too expensive. Interest has revived because rocket launches have become cheaper and the need for clean energy has grown. In 2023, a California Institute of Technology experiment called MAPLE became the first to beam power wirelessly in space and detect it on Earth. Japan, China, the United Kingdom and the European Space Agency are studying or planning demonstrations. Critics point to the enormous size and cost of power stations, the difficulty of building them in orbit and concerns about beam safety.",
s:[["How it would work","A solar power satellite in geostationary orbit, 36,000 kilometers up, would stay above one place on Earth. It would convert sunlight into electricity and then into a microwave beam aimed at a ground station called a rectenna, which could cover several square kilometers."],["Safety","Proposed microwave beams would be spread out and relatively weak, less intense than midday sunlight even at their center, so birds and planes passing through would not be harmed. Systems would include safeguards to shut off the beam if it wandered off target."],["Building in space","A useful power station might need solar arrays kilometers across, far larger than the International Space Station. Engineers propose building them robotically from many identical modules launched by reusable rockets."],["Advantages and doubts","Supporters say space solar power could provide steady, clean energy day and night and be directed to where it is needed, such as disaster zones. Skeptics argue that ground-based solar panels with batteries are becoming so cheap that space power may never compete."]],
k:[["Early idea","Isaac Asimov, ‘Reason’ (1941)"],["Patent","Peter Glaser, 1973"],["Transmission","Microwaves or lasers"],["First power beamed in space","Caltech MAPLE, 2023"],["Status","Experimental"]],
d:[["1941","Asimov’s story imagines a solar power station"],["1968","Peter Glaser proposes solar power satellites"],["1973","Glaser’s patent granted"],["1979","NASA and Energy Department complete a major study"],["2023","Caltech beams power in space"]],
s2:[["Japan’s plans","Japan has researched space solar power for decades and has tested microwave power transmission on the ground, with plans for small demonstrations from orbit."],["Power for the Moon","Similar technology could beam power to rovers and bases in the dark craters near the Moon’s poles, where sunlight never reaches."]],
sp:["Peter Glaser","Peter Glaser was a Czech-born American engineer. His 1968 article in the journal Science started serious study of space-based solar power."],
g:[["Rectenna","A receiving antenna that converts microwaves into electricity"],["Geostationary orbit","An orbit that keeps a satellite over one spot"],["Microwave","A type of radio wave"],["Wireless power","Sending energy without wires"]],
f:["Space solar panels can collect sunlight almost all the time.","The idea appeared in a 1941 story.","Caltech beamed power in space in 2023."],
fun:"The idea of beaming solar power from space first appeared in a science fiction story by Isaac Asimov in 1941."});

ADDART({id:"shoemakerlevy9",t:"Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💥",w:"Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9",r:["jupiter","comet","planetdefense","galileoprobe","hubblest"],
p:[["A comet hits Jupiter","In July 1994, pieces of a comet crashed into Jupiter."],["Torn apart","Jupiter’s gravity had broken the comet into more than 20 pieces."],["Scars larger than Earth","The impacts left dark marks bigger than our planet."]],
l:"Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9 was a comet that broke apart and collided with Jupiter in July 1994, the first collision between two solar system bodies ever directly observed. It was discovered in March 1993 by astronomers Carolyn and Eugene Shoemaker and David Levy using a telescope at Palomar Observatory in California; it was the ninth short-period comet the team had found together. Unusually, the comet was orbiting Jupiter rather than the Sun, having been captured by the planet’s gravity decades earlier, and it appeared as a string of fragments, described as a ‘string of pearls’. Calculations showed that the comet had passed so close to Jupiter in July 1992 that tides had torn it apart, and that the fragments would strike Jupiter two years later. Between 16 and 22 July 1994, about 21 fragments hit Jupiter’s southern hemisphere at about 60 kilometers per second, creating fireballs and dark scars, some larger than Earth, that lasted for weeks. Telescopes worldwide, including the Hubble Space Telescope, watched, and the Galileo spacecraft saw the impacts directly. The event showed how large impacts shape planets and raised public awareness of the danger asteroids and comets pose to Earth.",
s:[["The impacts","The largest fragment, labeled G, struck on 18 July 1994 with energy estimated at about 6 million megatons of TNT, hundreds of times more than all the world’s nuclear weapons combined. Plumes rose thousands of kilometers above Jupiter’s clouds."],["Jupiter the protector?","Jupiter’s strong gravity captures or flings away many comets, and some scientists suggested it shields Earth from impacts. Others argue that Jupiter also sends some comets and asteroids toward the inner solar system, so its overall effect is debated."],["Planetary defense","The spectacular impacts helped persuade the US Congress in 1998 to direct NASA to find most large near-Earth asteroids, leading to modern planetary defense programs and missions such as DART."],["Learning about Jupiter","The impacts stirred up material from below Jupiter’s cloud tops, letting scientists detect chemicals such as sulfur and ammonia compounds and study how Jupiter’s atmosphere moves."]],
k:[["Discovered","March 1993, Palomar Observatory"],["Discoverers","Carolyn and Eugene Shoemaker, David Levy"],["Broken apart","July 1992 by Jupiter’s gravity"],["Impacts","16–22 July 1994"],["Fragments","About 21"]],
d:[["1992","Comet torn apart by Jupiter’s gravity"],["1993","Comet discovered"],["1994","Fragments strike Jupiter"],["1998","US Congress directs NASA to search for near-Earth asteroids"],["2009","Another object hits Jupiter, seen by an amateur astronomer"]],
s2:[["Eugene Shoemaker","Geologist Eugene Shoemaker proved that Meteor Crater in Arizona was made by an impact and trained Apollo astronauts in geology. After his death in 1997, some of his ashes were carried to the Moon by NASA’s Lunar Prospector, making him the only person whose remains rest there."],["Carolyn Shoemaker","Carolyn Shoemaker began searching for comets in her 50s and discovered 32 comets and hundreds of asteroids, at one time more comets than any other living person."]],
sp:["Not so rare","Since 1994, amateur astronomers have recorded several smaller objects hitting Jupiter, appearing as brief flashes, showing that the giant planet is struck far more often than once thought."],
g:[["Comet","An icy body that grows a tail near the Sun"],["Tidal forces","Stretching forces caused by gravity"],["Fragment","A broken piece"],["Megaton","Energy equal to a million tons of TNT"]],
f:["Shoemaker–Levy 9 hit Jupiter in July 1994.","Jupiter’s gravity broke it into about 21 pieces.","Some impact scars were larger than Earth."],
fun:"Eugene Shoemaker, co-discoverer of the comet, is the only person whose ashes rest on the Moon."});
